<main id="spring-boot-ci-pipeline-page" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/Jenkins/Views/Shared/JenkinsTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- PAGE TITLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h1 class="fw-bold">Spring Boot CI Pipeline</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Learn how to design an enterprise-grade Continuous Integration pipeline for Spring Boot applications, covering execution wrappers, compilation verification, profile management, and fat-JAR packaging.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">What is a Spring Boot CI Pipeline?</h2>
                <p>
                    Spring Boot is a dominant microservices framework in the Enterprise Java ecosystem. A dedicated <strong>Spring Boot Continuous Integration (CI) Pipeline</strong> automates compliance testing and compilation constraints as developers commit new features.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Unlike standard foundational Java builds, a Spring Boot pipeline validates specialized app initializations. It targets native integration tests (like <code>@@SpringBootTest</code> context verification), maps profiles (such as <code>application-test.yml</code>), and bundles embedded servlet containers into a single self-contained executable file (executable fat-JAR).
                </p>
                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong> A specialized automation sequence in Jenkins that compiles Spring Java code, boots test contexts to confirm configuration safety, and packs a standalone executable with its container layers.
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- KEY CONCEPTS -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Key Concepts</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Maven/Gradle Wrappers</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Utilizing localized repository binaries (<code>./mvnw</code> or <code>./gradlew</code>) inside pipeline commands to guarantee the exact build runner engine version is pinned directly in source control.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Application Test Contexts</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Invoking the integration testing engine to verify that the Spring Dependency Injection container bootstraps successfully without runtime configuration syntax bugs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Spring Profile Injections</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Passing dynamic parameter variables (such as <code>-Dspring.profiles.active=ci</code>) during pipeline step execution to swap production cloud configurations with isolated test mocks.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Spring Boot Maven Plugin</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Executing the repackage goal via the build step to bundle your application bytecode along with runtime dependencies inside a single transportable archive.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EXAMPLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practical Jenkins Example</h2>
                <p>This declarative pipeline relies on the standard Maven wrapper (<code>./mvnw</code>) embedded within a repository to fetch dependencies, inject a dedicated build profile, and compile an executable Spring Boot application file:</p>
                <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any















    tools {
        // Sets up the base JDK version required to execute the framework context
        jdk 'OpenJDK-17'
    }

    stages {
        stage('Checkout & Lint') {
            steps {
                echo 'Verifying environment permissions...'
                // Ensures execution scripts have file permissions on Linux/macOS agents
                sh 'chmod +x mvnw'
                sh './mvnw --version'
            }
        }

        stage('Execute Core Unit Tests') {
            steps {
                echo 'Running unit tests and bootstrapping application contexts...'
                // Forces active profiles to pull isolated properties definitions
                sh './mvnw clean test -Dspring.profiles.active=ci'
            }
        }

        stage('Package Fat-JAR') {
            steps {
                echo 'Repackaging application into a self-contained runtime archive...'
                // Skips tests here since validation was accomplished during the previous stage
                sh './mvnw package -DskipTests'
            }
        }


        stage('Publish Distribution Binary') {
            steps {
                echo 'Stash build archive for subsequent continuous deployment phases...'
                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar', allowEmptyArchive: false, fingerprint: true
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- PRACTICE -->
<div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practice Exercise</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li class="mb-2">Verify your Spring Boot code repository includes standard wrapper utilities (such as <code>mvnw</code> and the <code>.mvn/</code> wrapper configurations folder).</li>
                    <li class="mb-2">Create a new <strong>Pipeline</strong> job in your Jenkins portal called <code>springboot-ci-pipeline</code>.</li>
                    <li class="mb-2">Paste the declarative wrapper implementation listed above into your pipeline configuration box.</li>
                    <li class="mb-2">Trigger a new build execution, view the <strong>Console Output</strong>, and track the process as it resolves structural assets and completes the Spring Boot repackage routine.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <!-- SUMMARY -->
            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
                <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">You have completed the <strong>Spring Boot CI Pipeline</strong> lesson. Automating the packaging mechanics of microservices allows you to expand into test results rendering and standard deployment frameworks.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- NAVIGATION -->
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="@Url.Action("Maven", "LanguageStacks", new { area = "Jenkins" })">← Previous: Java and Maven Builds</a>
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="@Url.Action("JUnitReports", "LanguageStacks", new { area = "Jenkins" })">Next: JUnit Test Reports →</a>
            </div>

        </section>

    </div>

</main>
